<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sanskrit">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanskrit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanskrit is a classical Indo-Aryan language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, used for religious, literary, philosophical and scientific texts over more than three millennia. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> classical Sanskrit literature reached its peak, with poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>. It was also adopted as a language of royal inscriptions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> note that Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> translated works from Sanskrit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanskrit.jpg" alt="Sanskrit" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
